  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at height 0.5 × loss over x in [-2, 2], z in [-3, 3]. Steeper along x, so with learning rate above 2/3 the swings across x grow and the run diverges.",
    "Each step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise per direction has spread 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b: 2 at b = 1, exactly 0 at b = 64.",
    "Both arrows are steps drawn at 3 times their length, one shared scale; the purple one is the step the ball takes next, the dark one the full-data step. The step-length readout is the purple step's true length.",
    "Diverged: loss above 6 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl; the run stops. Points off the bowl are drawn at its rim. Otherwise a run finishes after 40 steps.",
    "The trail holds the start point plus one point per step (up to 41). New run continues the seeded noise sequence; Reset replays it from the start.",
    "Steps play at 5 per second at speed 1. With noise, a single step lowers the loss only on average."
  ]
}